{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgRZArJZqsDCc6yEKHBzN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DSvogr1xbjPpsUNpFxpGvq","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"fix_prompt":"# Repair plan for /playwright/playwright:<startup>\n\n## Diagnosis\n\nThe failing \"test\" is not an assertion failure — it is the Playwright **suite\nstartup** failing. `tests/playwright.config.ts` declares a `webServer`:\n\n- `command: docker compose up --build frontend`\n- `cwd: '..'` (the workspace root)\n- `url: http://127.0.0.1:${port}` where `port` is derived from `process.cwd()`\n- `env: { FRONTEND_PORT: <port>, COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: <project> }`\n- `timeout: 300_000`, `reuseExistingServer: true`\n\nand the root `docker-compose.yaml` defines one service:\n\n```\nservices:\n  frontend:\n    build:\n      context: ./services/frontend\n    ports:\n      - \"${FRONTEND_PORT:-3000}:3000\"\n```\n\n`services/` does not exist in this workspace. `docker compose up --build\nfrontend` therefore fails immediately on a missing build context, the\n`webServer` never answers on `http://127.0.0.1:<port>`, and Playwright aborts\nthe run with a startup error — which is why the failure group is empty and the\ntest id is `<startup>` rather than the spec's test name.\n\nThe whole repair is: **create the missing Next.js frontend service under\n`services/frontend/`** so that `docker compose up --build frontend` produces a\ncontainer listening on port `3000` internally and serving `/` with a single\n`<h1>Hello world</h1>`.\n\nThis is entirely inside the declared edit root (`services/frontend`) and inside\nthe allowed write globs (`Write(services/**)`). Nothing outside `services/`\nneeds to change: do **not** touch `tests/`, `docker-compose.yaml`,\n`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `true-bdd/`.\n\n## Contract the new service must satisfy\n\nDerived from the harness, compose file and architecture doc — these are the\nhard constraints, not preferences:\n\n1. **Build context is `services/frontend` itself.** Everything the image needs\n   must live under that directory; the Dockerfile must be at\n   `services/frontend/Dockerfile` and must only `COPY` paths relative to that\n   directory (no `../` escapes — compose passes only that directory as context).\n2. **The app listens on TCP `3000` inside the container, on `0.0.0.0`.** Compose\n   maps `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000`; binding to `127.0.0.1` inside the container\n   would make the published port unreachable and reproduce the same startup\n   timeout.\n3. **`GET /` returns an HTML page with exactly one `<h1>` whose normalised text\n   is `Hello world`.** `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` does\n   `page.locator('h1')` + `toHaveText('Hello world')`: the locator is strict, so\n   a second `<h1>` anywhere in the document (including in the root layout, a\n   header, or an error overlay) fails with a strict-mode violation, and\n   `toHaveText` with a string is an exact match after whitespace normalisation —\n   no extra words, no suffix, no `Hello World` casing.\n4. **Cold start must complete well inside the 300 s `webServer` timeout**, which\n   covers `docker build` *and* container boot. Keep the dependency set minimal\n   and the image layers cache-friendly.\n5. **Next.js App Router with server components by default**, per the\n   `component-composition` pattern in `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`.\n   The home page needs no hooks and no browser APIs, so it must **not** carry\n   `'use client'`.\n6. **`services/frontend/tsconfig.json` must exist and type-check**, because\n   `architecture.yaml` declares it as the `typescript` code quality gate.\n\n## Files to create\n\nAll paths are relative to the workspace root.\n\n### 1. `services/frontend/package.json`\nServes: the `<startup>` group (image build) and, transitively, the home-page spec.\n\n- Private, named package (e.g. `frontend`), `\"private\": true`.\n- Dependencies: `next`, `react`, `react-dom`. Prefer a conservative, widely\n  available pairing — Next `14.2.x` with React `18.3.x` — over the newest\n  major, to minimise the risk of a resolution or peer-dependency failure\n  inside the container build. If you choose Next 15, pair it with React 19.\n- Dev dependencies: `typescript`, `@types/node`, `@types/react`,\n  `@types/react-dom` — these are needed at build time because `next build`\n  type-checks, so the Dockerfile must install dev dependencies (do not pass\n  `--omit=dev` to the install step that precedes the build).\n- Scripts: `dev` (`next dev`), `build` (`next build`), `start`\n  (`next start`), and optionally `typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`) for the\n  quality gate.\n- **Do not reference a lockfile you cannot produce.** If you cannot run\n  `npm install` to generate `services/frontend/package-lock.json`, the\n  Dockerfile must use `npm install`, not `npm ci` — `npm ci` hard-fails\n  without a lockfile and would reproduce the startup failure. If you *can*\n  generate a lockfile, commit it and use `npm ci` for determinism.\n\n### 2. `services/frontend/tsconfig.json`\nServes: the `typescript` quality gate declared in `architecture.yaml`; also\nguards the in-container `next build` type-check that gates the `<startup>` group.\n\nStandard Next.js App Router configuration: `target` ES2017 or later,\n`lib` including `dom`, `dom.iterable`, `esnext`; `allowJs`, `skipLibCheck`,\n`strict: true`, `noEmit: true`, `esModuleInterop`, `module: esnext`,\n`moduleResolution: bundler` (or `node` if you pin an older TS),\n`resolveJsonModule`, `isolatedModules`, `jsx: preserve`, `incremental: true`,\nthe `next` TS plugin, and a `@/*` path alias rooted at the service directory if\nyou use one. `include` must cover `next-env.d.ts`, `**/*.ts`, `**/*.tsx` and\n`.next/types/**/*.ts`; `exclude` `node_modules`.\n\n### 3. `services/frontend/next-env.d.ts`\nServes: the quality gate.\n\nNext regenerates this during `next build`, but committing it means a host-side\n`tsc -p services/frontend/tsconfig.json` resolves Next's JSX and image types\nwithout a prior build. Keep it to the two standard reference directives and the\n\"do not edit\" comment; do not add declarations of your own.\n\n### 4. `services/frontend/app/layout.tsx`\nServes: the home-page spec (App Router requires a root layout; without one the\nroute 500s and the `h1` assertion never sees a page).\n\nA server component (no `'use client'`) default-exporting `RootLayout({\nchildren })` that renders `<html lang=\"en\">` wrapping `<body>{children}</body>`.\nOptionally export `metadata` with a title/description. **It must not render any\nheading element** — any `<h1>` here would collide with the page's heading and\nbreak the strict locator (constraint 3).\n\n### 5. `services/frontend/app/page.tsx`\nServes: `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` — \"home page greets with\nHello world\".\n\nA server component default-exporting the `/` page. It renders a single `<h1>`\nwhose only child is the literal text `Hello world`. Wrap it in a `<main>` if you\nlike; keep the heading's text node free of surrounding markup that would add\nextra text inside the `h1` (no nested spans with extra copy, no punctuation, no\ntrailing exclamation mark). Do not add a second heading of level 1 anywhere on\nthe page.\n\n### 6. `services/frontend/Dockerfile`\nServes: the `<startup>` group directly — this is the file whose absence is the\nroot cause.\n\nIntended shape (describe-then-implement; keep it simple and deterministic):\n\n- Base on a pinned Node 20 image (e.g. `node:20-alpine`). Alpine keeps the\n  pull and build inside the 300 s budget; if you hit native-module issues,\n  fall back to `node:20-slim`.\n- `WORKDIR /app`.\n- Set `NEXT_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1` so the first build never blocks on the\n  telemetry notice or a telemetry network call.\n- Copy `package.json` (and `package-lock.json` if you generated one) first and\n  run the install step, so dependency installation is cached indep