hich is why the failure group is empty and the\ntest id is `<startup>` rather than the spec's test name.\n\nThe whole repair is: **create the missing Next.js frontend service under\n`services/frontend/`** so that `docker compose up --build frontend` produces a\ncontainer listening on port `3000` internally and serving `/` with a single\n`<h1>Hello world</h1>`.\n\nThis is entirely inside the declared edit root (`services/frontend`) and inside\nthe allowed write globs (`Write(services/**)`). Nothing outside `services/`\nneeds to change: do **not** touch `tests/`, `docker-compose.yaml`,\n`docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`, `CLAUDE.md`, or `true-bdd/`.\n\n## Contract the new service must satisfy\n\nDerived from the harness, compose file and architecture doc — these are the\nhard constraints, not preferences:\n\n1. **Build context is `services/frontend` itself.** Everything the image needs\n   must live under that directory; the Dockerfile must be at\n   `services/frontend/Dockerfile` and must only `COPY` paths relative to that\n   directory (no `../` escapes — compose passes only that directory as context).\n2. **The app listens on TCP `3000` inside the container, on `0.0.0.0`.** Compose\n   maps `${FRONTEND_PORT}:3000`; binding to `127.0.0.1` inside the container\n   would make the published port unreachable and reproduce the same startup\n   timeout.\n3. **`GET /` returns an HTML page with exactly one `<h1>` whose normalised text\n   is `Hello world`.** `tests/integration/frontend-home.spec.ts` does\n   `page.locator('h1')` + `toHaveText('Hello world')`: the locator is strict, so\n   a second `<h1>` anywhere in the document (including in the root layout, a\n   header, or an error overlay) fails with a strict-mode violation, and\n   `toHaveText` with a string is an exact match after whitespace normalisation —\n   no extra words, no suffix, no `Hello World` casing.\n4. **Cold start must complete well inside the 300 s `webServer` timeout**, which\n   covers `docker bu